My current finding is that the “144‑functions’’ answer is incorrect because any admissible set of cyclotomic factors must have a **common 2‑adic exponent**; otherwise the factors cannot all divide the same binomial \(1+x^{d}\).  The even‑indexed cyclotomic polynomials with \(\varphi\le8\) are  

\[
\Phi_{2},\Phi_{6},\Phi_{10},\Phi_{14},\Phi_{18},\Phi_{30}\;(v_{2}=1),\;
\Phi_{4},\Phi_{12},\Phi_{20}\;(v_{2}=2),\;
\Phi_{8},\Phi_{24}\;(v_{2}=3),\;
\Phi_{16}\;(v_{2}=4).
\]

Only subsets drawn from a single \(v_{2}\)‑group may appear.  Enumerating all such subsets whose total degree \(\sum\varphi\le8\) gives 22 admissible subsets (including the empty set); their contributions \(\sum(9-\deg)\) equal \(80\).  Multiplying by the two possible signs yields  

\[
{160}
\]

shifty functions.  This disproves both the 144‑count and the 266‑count, which ignored the \(v_{2}\)‑consistency condition.
